1038	\begin{definition}\label{def:ith-restricted-line}
1039	  For any $i \in \{1, \dots,
1040	  m\}$ the \emph{$i$-th restricted axis parallel line distribution $D_{\ALine,
1041	    i}$} is the restriction of $D_{\ALine}$ to pairs  $(\line, u)$
1042	  where the line $\line$ is along the direction $e_i$ (i.e. is of the
1043	  form $\line = (u_0, e_i)$. Similarly, the \emph{$i$-th restricted
1044	  diagonal line distribution $D_{\DLine, i}$} is the restriction of
1045	  $D_{\DLine}$ to pairs $(\line, u)$, where the line $\line = (u_0,
1046	  s, v)$ is such that $\chi(s) = i$, and coordinates $1, \dots, i-1$ of
1047	  $v$ are $0$. (Recall that $\chi$ is defined in~\eqref{eq:chi-func}.)
1048	\end{definition}
1049	  Observe that $D_{\ALine}$ is a uniform mixture over the
1050	  distributions $D_{\ALine, i}$ for $i \in \{1, \dots, m\}$, and
1051	  likewise for $D_{\DLine}$.
1052	  As a consequence, any approximation that
1053	  holds on average over the line-point distribution $D_{\Line}$ with
1054	  error $\delta$ holds over each of the $i$-th restricted distributions
1055	  $D_{\ALine, i}$ and $D_{\DLine, i}$ with error $2m\delta$. Similarly,
1056	  any approximation that holds over $D_{\Line} \times D_{\Line}$ holds
1057	  over any product of the $i$-th restricted distributions with error
1058	  $4m^2 \delta$. For example, by \Cref{lem:qld-xz-lines} for any $\tvar_1, \tvar_2 \in \{\ALine, \DLine\}$ and $i, j \in \{1, \dots, m\}$, we have
1059	  \begin{equation}
